  style="height: 80vh; overflow-y: auto;"
  {{on "scroll" this.handleScroll}}
>
  {{#each this.model as |post|}}
    <article class="post">
      <h2>{{post.title}}</h2>
      <p>{{post.excerpt}}</p>
    </article>
  {{/each}}

  {{#if this.controller.loading}}
    <div class="loading">Loading…</div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#unless this.controller.hasMore}}
    <div class="end-of-list">No more posts</div>
  {{/unless}}
</div>
```

**How it works**

1. The route loads the first page of posts.  
2. The controller keeps track of the current page, whether a load is in progress, and if more pages exist.  
3. The `infinite-scroll` component listens for the `scroll` event on the container.  
4. When the user scrolls near the bottom, it calls the controller’s `debouncedLoadMore` task, which in turn calls `loadMore`.  
5. `loadMore` queries the next page; Ember Data merges the new records into the existing array, so the template automatically updates.  
6. When a page returns fewer records than requested, `hasMore` is set to `false` and the “No more posts” message is shown.

