response_prompt:
`next-auth` only puts a **very small** amount of data into the session by default.  
When you use the **JWT** strategy the session is created from the token that is
stored in the cookie.  
The token only contains the fields that you explicitly add in the
`jwt` callback, and the session only contains the fields that you expose in
the `session` callback.

In your `authorize` function you return a Mongoose document (`user`).  
That document contains many fields, but only the `_id` and `email` are
automatically copied into the JWT.  
Everything else is discarded unless you add it yourself.

### What you need to do

1. **Return a plain object** from `authorize` (so that NextAuth can read it).  
2. **Add a `jwt` callback** that copies the fields you want into the token.  
3. **Add a `session` callback** that copies the fields from the token into the
   session object that the client receives.

```js
// [...nextauth].js
import NextAuth from "next-auth";
import CredentialsProvider from "next-auth/providers/credentials";
import { connectToDb } from "@utils/database";
import User from "@models/user";
import bcrypt from "bcrypt";

export const authOptions = {
  providers: [
    CredentialsProvider({
      name: "credentials",
      credentials: {
        email: { label: "Email", type: "email", placeholder: "" },
        password: { label: "Password", type: "password", placeholder: "" },
      },

      async authorize(credentials) {
        const { email, password } = credentials;

        try {
          await connectToDb();

          const user = await User.findOne({ email });

          if (!user) return null;

          const passwordsMatch = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password);
          if (!passwordsMatch) return null;

          // Return a plain JS object (no Mongoose methods)
          return {
            id: user._id.toString(),
            email: user.email,
            firstName: user.firstName,
            lastName: user.lastName,
            phoneNumber: user.phoneNumber,
          };
        } catch (error) {
          console.error(error);
          return null;
        }
      },
    }),
  ],

  // Tell next-auth to use JWTs for the session
  session: { strategy: "jwt" },

  // Add callbacks to shape the token and session
  callbacks: {
    // Called whenever a JWT is created or updated
    async jwt({ token, user }) {
      // On sign‑in `user` is defined
      if (user) {
        token.id = user.id;
        token.email = user.email;
        token.firstName = user.firstName;
        token.lastName = user.lastName;
        token.phoneNumber = user.phoneNumber;
      }
      return token;
    },

    // Called whenever a session is created (e.g. on the client)
    async session({ session, token }) {
      // Copy the fields from the token into the session
      session.user = {
        id: token.id,
        email: token.email,
        firstName: token.firstName,
        lastName: token.lastName,
        phoneNumber: token.phoneNumber,
      };
      return session;
    },
  },

  secret: process.env.NEXTAUTH_SECRET,
  pages: { signIn: "/login" },
};

const handler = NextAuth(authOptions);
export { handler as GET, handler as POST };
```

### Using the data on the client

```tsx
// DashboardPage.tsx
'use client';

import { useSession } from "next-auth/react";

export default function DashboardPage() {
  const { data: session, status } = useSession();

  console.log("session", session); // now contains all the fields

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      {status === "loading" ? (
        <p>Loading…</p>
      ) : (
        <>
          <p>Email: {session?.user?.email}</p>
          <p>Name: {session?.user?.firstName} {session?.user?.lastName}</p>
          <p>Phone: {session?.user?.phoneNumber}</p>
        </>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

With the callbacks in place, the session object that the client receives will
contain `email`, `firstName`, `lastName`, `phoneNumber`, and any other fields
you add. The password is never sent to the client, which is the intended
behaviour.

rag_response:
`next-auth` only puts a **very small** set of fields into the session by default.  
When you use the **JWT** strategy the `authorize()` callback returns a user
document, but the library then runs the `jwt` and `session` callbacks.  
The default `session` callback only copies `name`, `email` and `image` from the
token, so everything else (firstName, lastName, phoneNumber, …) is dropped.

To keep the extra fields you have to add them yourself in the callbacks.

```ts
// pages/api/auth/[...nextauth].ts
import NextAuth from "next-auth";
import CredentialsProvider from "next-auth/providers/credentials";
import { connectToDb } from "@utils/database";
import User from "@models/user";
import bcrypt from "bcrypt";

export const authOptions = {
  providers: [
    CredentialsProvider({
      name: "credentials",
      credentials: {
        email: { label: "Email", type: "email", placeholder: "" },
        password: { label: "Password", type: "password", placeholder: "" },
      },

      async authorize(credentials) {
        const { email, password } = credentials || {};

        try {
          await connectToDb();

          const user = await User.findOne({ email });
          if (!user) return null;

          const passwordsMatch = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password);
          if (!passwordsMatch) return null;

          // Return the whole user document (or a subset you want)
          return {
            id: user._id.toString(),
            email: user.email,
            firstName: user.firstName,
            lastName: user.lastName,
            phoneNumber: user.phoneNumber,
          };
        } catch (error) {
          console.error(error);
          return null;
        }
      },
    }),
  ],

  // Use JWT so we can store the data in the token
  session: { strategy: "jwt" },

  callbacks: {
    // Add the user data to the JWT token
    async jwt({ token, user }) {
      if (user) {
        token.id = user.id;
        token.firstName = user.firstName;
        token.lastName = user.lastName;
        token.phoneNumber = user.phoneNumber;
      }
      return token;
    },

    // Expose the data from the token to the session
    async session({ session, token }) {
      session.user = {
        id: token.id,
        email: token.email,
        firstName: token.firstName,
        lastName: token.lastName,
        phoneNumber: token.phoneNumber,
      };
      return session;
    },
  },

  secret: process.env.NEXTAUTH_SECRET,
  pages: { signIn: "/login" },
};

const handler = NextAuth(authOptions);
export { handler as GET, handler as POST };
```

Now in your dashboard you can read all the fields:

```tsx
'use client';
import { useSession } from "next-auth/react";

export default function DashboardPage() {
  const { data: session, status } = useSession();

  console.log("session", session);

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      {session?.user?.email && <p>Email: {session.user.email}</p>}
      {session?.user?.firstName && <p>First name: {session.user.firstName}</p>}
      {session?.user?.lastName && <p>Last name: {session.user.lastName}</p>}
      {session?.user?.phoneNumber && <p>Phone: {session.user.phoneNumber}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Key points**

1. **Default session callback** only returns `name`, `email`, and `image`.  
2. Use the `jwt` callback to copy any fields you need into the token.  
3. Use the `session` callback to expose those fields to the client.  
4. Never expose sensitive data (e.g., passwords) in the token or session.

